#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048557 :topic/difficulty 0.7069117600446577 :topic/id #uuid "a9814439-0881-561b-9a31-726d94821935" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064206 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064077 :question/id #uuid "d6501a20-6ebd-5786-870e-3527423b6332" :question/math-academy-id "e-449" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{5x+2}\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=5x+2.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 5 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{5x+2}\\text{d}x & =3\\int \\frac{1}{5x+2}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|5x+2|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc4294d6-3d60-5c9e-a862-863e1d129049" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064207 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064078 :question/id #uuid "115b3fb1-1041-532a-b5fa-4d8a53c9ab2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-450" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x},$ writing your final answer as a single function." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=1+2x.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d x = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1+2x}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C = \\dfrac 1 2\\ln K$ for another arbitrary constant $K > 0,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K\\cdot |1+2x|) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|1+2x|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{1+2x}\\textrm{d}x  = \\dfrac 1 2 \\ln \\left(K |1+2x|\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064208 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064079 :question/id #uuid "2c4912bd-290a-5074-b11b-459c77b7e238" :question/math-academy-id "e-451" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int\\left(\\dfrac{1}{1+2x} + \\dfrac{1}{1-2x}\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's solve this using the general formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{1}{1+2x}+\\frac{1}{1-2x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{1+2x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\frac{1}{1-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1+2x|-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1-2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1+2x}{1-2x}|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|\\frac{1+2x}{1-2x}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the laws of logarithms to write the result as a single logarithm."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af32f07f-fa65-5ede-a506-735dc2d3a5ef" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Combination of Linear Rational Functions Using the General Formula"}] :topic/math-academy-id 331 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050771}] :topic/title "Integrating Linear Rational Functions Using Substitution"}]}
